ss and you know uh we we saw that  particularly with the cameron government  which um i think that was fairly closely  acquainted with um  which you know adopted a policy of  complete appeasement in a basement to  china and was lavishly praised amongst  others by the global times for doing so  and and essentially told yes that's how  we want foreigners to behave um i don't  think it did anybody very much good to  be honest  but what i find  most puzzling about this argument is i'm  not quite sure how not cooperating on  china improves human right and not  cooperating on climate improves human  rights in china or indeed improves any  of the other ills that we wish to  address  it it makes no difference  well i think it it's it it's more a  question of whether or not uh in our  pursuit of climate we are letting that  get a climate agreements we are letting  that get in the way of other interests  and and i think that does happen and i  think you know the the degree to which  uh the the conference of the parties in  glasgow  um has  uh  partly because it's been delayed for a  year um uh intruded on almost every uh  diplomatic conversation over the last  year or two  is problematic we know that the glasgow  conference is not going to achieve very  much we know that because it's the 26th  such meeting and the previous 25 haven't  solved the problem and they've come up  with some extraordinary slights of hands  to sort of pretend they're solving the  problem i mean the paris agreement  basically came up with a non  it insisted it was going to come up with  a binding treaty in fact it came up with  a binding treaty to have non-binding  promises which is a sort of slight of  hand  and the you know the cancun agreement  came up with a  an agreement to create but not to fund a  fund of climate  we're going to have some similar sort of  compromise out of glasgow is it really  worth it uh to have these tens of  thousands of people descend descending  upon britain to have this  